  {
    "reasoning": "The dominant failure is systematic bias against meta-options. The agent finds a plausible concrete answer and then rationalizes away the meta-option. Two edits address this: (1) explicit meta-option handling guidance, (2) a caution against fabricating theorem-level knowledge to rank options. These do not hardcode any paper-specific content.",
    "edits": [
      {
        "op": "insert_after",
        "target": "## Option Comparison",
        "content": "\n### Meta-Options (\"One is correct, but stronger exists\")\n- When a meta-option states that another option is correct but a stronger result can be proven, treat it as a serious candidate, not a last resort. These questions test whether you know the *full* strength of a result.\n- Do NOT dismiss the meta-option just because a concrete option looks correct. The meta-option wins whenever the concrete option is true but *understates* the theorem (e.g., omits additional properties of a function like monotonicity/convexity/boundary behavior, or states only one direction of a proven equivalence, or gives a weaker quantifier than what holds).\n- Default heuristic: if the question asks for the \"strongest statement\" and you are uncertain whether the best concrete option captures every provable conclusion, prefer the meta-option over a concrete option you are not fully sure is maximal.",
